course: dictionary
lesson: 4233
title: Grandchild: a family word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A family word tells you how people are related to each other. A grandchild is a child of your son or daughter. «Calls from grandchildren mean a lot to grandparents far away.» The grandparents are the ones receiving the calls, because the grandchildren are their sons' and daughters' children.
explain: Context shows you that grandchild can mean a boy or a girl. «Lots of grandparents spoil their grandchildren: they often have more time and fewer rules than busy parents.» Spoil means to give lots of treats, and the grandchildren are the ones being spoiled, whoever they are.
explain: The word before grandchild can add a clue. Great- means one more step in the family. «The letters you are reading now are its great-grandchildren» means they come from a much older idea, several generations back. Look for great- and you know the family line is getting longer.
fact: A grandchild is a child of your son or daughter.
fact: A grandson is a boy grandchild, and a granddaughter is a girl grandchild.
fact: The plural of grandchild is grandchildren.
fact: Great- in front of a family word means one more generation: a great-grandchild is the child of a grandchild.
fact: In a family tree, grandchildren are two generations below the grandparents.
exercise: «Calls from grandchildren mean a lot to grandparents far away.» Who is making the calls? | The grandparents | The parents | *The grandchildren | The monarchs
exercise: A grandchild is a child of whom? | Your uncle or aunt | *Your son or daughter | Your brother or sister | Your cousin
exercise: «Each of her 3 children has 2 children.» How many grandchildren does she have in total? | *6 | 5 | 3 | 2
test: What is the plural of grandchild? | grandchild | *grandchildren | grandchildes | grandchilds
test: Which word means a girl grandchild? | grandson | *granddaughter | grandparent | great-aunt
exam: Which family word is two generations below the grandparents? | Great-grandchild | *Grandchild | Parent | Grandparent
